text:
Names are only matched by exact spelling right now, so I'll make `require_member` look names up ignoring case and return the stored spelling, then route every place a name comes in through it.

Every name from the command line now goes through `require_member`. Now the tests:

Names now ignore case everywhere, and output always uses the spelling from when the person was first added. All 32 tests pass, including 7 new ones for this.

**What changed** (`kitty/core.py`)
- **One lookup for names.** Every command turns a typed name into the stored spelling through a single lookup that ignores case and spaces at either end: the payer, the `--among`, `--exact`, `--shares` and `--percent` names, both people in a payment, `history`, and `member remove`. Expenses and payments then record the stored spelling, so `expense add alice …` saves "Alice".
- **`member add alice`** fails if "Alice" already exists. The error shows the existing spelling: `'Alice' is already a member`.
- **Listing the same person twice** in different cases is caught. `--among alice,ALICE` gives "listed more than once", and `payment add alice ALICE 5` gives "needs two different people".
- **Messages use the stored spelling too:** `member remove CARA` prints `Removed Cara.`
- **Tie-breaking:** I removed the extra tie-break I described last time, since two names that differ only in case can no longer exist.

**The new tests** add Alice, bob and Cara, then use mixed-case spellings with every command that takes a name. They check that:
- `member add` rejects the same name in another case;
- `member remove` works in any case;
- the payer and every split option accept any case;
- entering the same person twice in different cases is rejected;
- a misspelled name is still an unknown member;
- payments, `balances`, `settle`, `report`, `history` (including its "Payment to/from" lines) and the CSV export all show the original spelling.
